course: money
lesson: 15
title: Taking without paying: the consequences
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Taking something from a shop without paying is called shoplifting, and it is stealing, even if it is only one 80p chocolate bar or one grape from the fruit pile.
explain: When Zak's mate dares him to pocket a bar, Zak says no and walks out: getting caught can mean a ban from the shop, a call to parents, or the police, and a young person can get a warning that stays on a record.
explain: Stealing hurts everyone: if 5 bars at 60p go missing every day, the shop loses £3 a day, which is £90 in 30 days, so shops raise prices a little and make rules like "only 2 school children at a time", even for honest kids.
explain: Mistakes can be put right: when a 4-year-old walks out holding a toy he didn't understand he had to pay for, Mum takes it straight back and says sorry, and when Kai finds a £1 coin on the floor he hands it in.
fact: Shoplifting means taking things from a shop without paying; it is stealing.
fact: Even small things count, including eating food in a shop before paying for it, unless it is a free sample you are offered.
fact: Shops have cameras, security guards and staff who watch for stealing.
fact: Consequences of shoplifting can include being banned from the shop, a call to parents, and the police; a warning can stay on a record.
fact: A ban from a shop can last months or years and makes everyday life harder.
fact: Shops cover the money they lose to stealing partly by raising prices, so honest customers pay too.
fact: Some shops make rules for everyone, like limiting how many school children come in at once, after things are stolen.
fact: Money you find may belong to someone who needs it; handing it in is the honest thing, and many shops keep lost property.
fact: A grown-up who asks a child to carry stolen things is using the child, and the child can get into serious trouble; say no and tell a trusted adult.
fact: Little children sometimes take things without understanding; a grown-up puts it right by taking the item back.
fact: A dare is a challenge to do something, often something risky or wrong; saying no to a bad dare takes courage.
fact: "Nicking" is British slang for stealing.
exercise: What is shoplifting? | Taking things from a shop without paying | Lifting heavy boxes of stock around a shop | Buying lots of things cheaply in a big sale | Returning broken things to a shop for a refund
exercise: Zak's mate dares him to pocket a chocolate bar. What is the right choice? | Say no and walk away | Do it, a bar is too small to matter | Take a smaller bar instead | Do it and pay next week
exercise: What can happen to someone caught shoplifting? | A ban from the shop, a call to parents, or the police | Nothing, if the item was cheap | The shop gives them a free bag | They get a reward for being quick
exercise: 5 bars at 60p go missing from a shop every day. How much does it lose each day? | £3 | £5 | 60p | £30
exercise: That shop loses £3 a day. How much is that in 30 days? | £90 | £30 | £33 | £60
exercise: Why does a shop put up a sign "Only 2 school children at a time"? | Things were stolen, so they made a rule for everyone | Shops don't like children and don't want them inside at all | The shop is too small for 3 people | It is the law in every shop
exercise: Ruby's 4-year-old brother walks out holding a £3 toy. What does Mum do? | Takes it straight back and says sorry | Keeps it, because he didn't mean to take it | Pays £6 later on, just to be on the safe side | Hides it in the car so nobody finds out
exercise: After the toy goes back, how much has the shop lost? | £0 | £3 | £6 | £1.50
exercise: A shop loses £100 a week to stealing and has 1,000 customers. How much extra does each customer pay to cover it? | 10p | £1 | 1p | £10
exercise: Kai finds a £1 coin on the shop floor. What is the honest thing? | Hand it to the till in case someone comes back | Keep it, because finders keepers is the rule in shops | Spend it on sweets straight away | Hide it under a shelf
exercise: A man offers Harry £2 to hide a bottle in his school bag and walk it out. What should Harry do? | Say no and go to his mum | Do it, £2 is a good deal | Do it, but only once | Ask for £5 instead
exercise: If Harry had been caught, who would be in trouble? | Harry, even though the man asked him | Only the man who asked, never Harry himself | Nobody, because it was only one cheap bottle | The shop, for leaving bottles where people can reach
exercise: One grape costs about 1p. If 100 people eat one each day, how much does the shop lose a day? | £1 | 1p | 10p | £100
exercise: Ben's cousin is banned from the supermarket 5 minutes away and walks 40 minutes to the other one. How many extra minutes each way? | 35 | 45 | 40 | 8
exercise: Why do honest people end up paying for stealing? | Shops raise prices a little to cover their losses | The police fine every customer a little each time they shop | Honest people must pay for cameras | They don't, it costs nobody anything
exercise: What is a trusted adult? | A grown-up you can talk to safely, like a parent or teacher | Any stranger who offers you money | A grown-up who asks you to keep secrets | A shop's security camera
test: Lola's mate goes "it's just one grape, innit." The shop man frowns. Who's right? | The man: eating food before paying is still taking | The mate: one grape doesn't count | Both: grapes are always free samples | Neither: grapes belong to everyone
test: Kai's mate shouts "finders keepers!" about the £1 coin. What's the better rule? | It might be someone's, so hand it in | Finders keepers, because that is the law in Britain | Keep it, as long as nobody saw you pick it up | Split it with your friend, 50p each, so it's fair
test: Jayden says "only 2 school kids at a time? That's harsh, man." His dad says "they've had stuff nicked, son." ("Nicked" means stolen.) Why is the rule there? | Stealing made the shop limit everyone | The shopkeeper hates kids | Kids are too loud in shops | The law says only 2 children anywhere
test: Ben's cousin moans "gotta walk 40 minutes now." Before it was 5 minutes. How many extra minutes is a trip there and back? | 70 | 35 | 45 | 80
test: What would happen if lots of people shoplifted from a small shop? | It could raise prices or even close, hurting everyone nearby | It would get richer and lower its prices | Nothing, shops have endless money | The government would pay for all the losses
test: A bloke by the door: "Stick this in your bag, kid, two quid for you." A Scottish lass nearby says "dinnae, hen, tell yer maw." ("Dinnae" means don't; "hen" is a friendly name for a girl; "maw" means mum.) What should the child do? | Refuse and tell their mum or a trusted adult | Do it, it's only two quid | Do it, but give the money back to the bloke later on | Ask the bloke for more money
test: Zak's mate laughs "you're such a chicken" when Zak won't take the bar. ("Chicken" means scared.) What's true? | Saying no to a bad dare takes real courage | Dares must always be done, or everyone will laugh at you | Taking it would prove he's grown up | Chickens are allowed to steal
test: A cartoon shows a cheeky kid grabbing sweets and running off, with no one caring. What's wrong with that? | In real life shops have cameras and staff, and it's stealing | Nothing, sweets are free for children | Real shops let anyone take things | Only grown-ups can be caught
test: Mia sees a teen stopped by a guard over a £5 charger. Mum: "They'll call the police and his parents." What else might happen? | He might be banned from shops in the centre | He gets the charger free, with just a warning letter | He gets offered a job as a security guard there | Nothing happens, because the charger was cheap
test: Dad: "When people nick stuff, we all pay a bit more." If a shop loses £50 a week and has 500 customers, how much extra each? | 10p | 50p | £1 | 1p
test: Ruby's little bro, aged 4, walked out with a toy. Is he a thief? | No, he didn't understand; Mum put it right by taking it back | Yes, and he should be banned for ever | Yes, the police must be called | No, because toys are free for small kids
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the act designated "shoplifting"? | The removal of goods from a shop without payment, constituting theft | The carrying of heavy merchandise within a shop by its staff | The purchase of goods at a reduced price during a sale | The return of goods to a shop for a refund
exam: It is given that five articles priced at sixty pence each are stolen daily from a shop. Compute the loss incurred over a period of thirty days. | Ninety pounds | Thirty pounds | Three pounds | Eighteen pounds
exam: Identify the course of action most consistent with the established guidance where an adult requests a child to conceal goods and remove them from a shop. | Refusal and the informing of a trusted adult | Compliance upon receipt of the payment offered | Compliance on a single occasion only | Negotiation of a larger payment
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the effect of theft upon the prices charged to honest customers. | Prices are raised in part to cover losses arising from theft | Prices are reduced in order to deter theft | Prices are unaffected by any losses whatsoever | Prices are paid in full by the police
